      "claim_excerpt": "The two-full border previously needed a missing exact boundary Gaussian or an unpriced extraction. This package gives a smaller, explicitly declared productive domain: L independent X inputs and L\u22121 independent Y inputs per main pair, all native helper inputs retained, and one owned terminal field. Prefix compression gives an exact noncommutative actual-inverse pair, with no exact boundary provider for this reduced-domain contract.\n\nThe local ideal-native invoice is `target=L*T\u2212112H17`, `paid=L*P`. Exact rational enclosures show L=66 passes the local C=.0034 screen with margin `[5.551473221217, 5.551473223332]`; L=65 fails. **This is not a new \u03ba or an admitted recursive supplier.** A conditional graded endpoint theorem supplies O(e) intermediate height; standard root-only Walsh averaging then extracts the exact Gaussian diagonal using a fixed number of complete executions, without adding repeated-input copies to productive credit.\n\nThe remaining gates are explicit: the actual native word must supply coherent shears with all helpers/frames/fresh D factors; a fixed graded whole-job compiler must preserve source/exit/complement ownership and the complete profile; physical/stock/BIT costs remain. An actual inverse by itself does not supply the shear structure. Naive Pascal iteration changes grade populations, so the one-shot invoice is not asserted at every recursive level. The full-domain boundary-provider comparison and scoped counterexamples are kept separate.\n\nValidation: seven bounded standard-library checkers pass directly on CPython 3.14.5 in normal and optimized modes w",
